findings.body:
This replaces safe `execFile` (no shell, argv array) with `exec` and interpolates `src` and `filter` into the command string. Both are attacker-influenced in a typical image pipeline: `filter` is described as user-composed, and `src` is only double-quoted, so values with `"`, `;`, `$()`, or backticks execute arbitrary shell on the host.

`-filter` does not need a shell — pass it as a separate argv element and allowlist the filter name:

```suggestion
import { execFile } from "child_process";

const ALLOWED_FILTERS = new Set(["Lanczos", "Mitchell", "Catrom", "Cubic", "Triangle"]);

export function resize(src: string, filter: string, w: number): Promise<string> {
  if (!ALLOWED_FILTERS.has(filter)) {
    return Promise.reject(new Error(`unsupported filter: ${filter}`));
  }
  return new Promise((res, rej) => {
    execFile("convert", [src, "-filter", filter, "-resize", `${w}x`, "/tmp/out.png"], (e) => e ? rej(e) : res("/tmp/out.png"));
  });
}
```
